oa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-4x3.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-4x3.webp","width":1200,"height":900,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","description":"Tall oceanfront towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach, with a row of more towers into the distance","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"DiscoA340"},"creditText":"DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg"},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-16x9.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-16x9.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","description":"Tall oceanfront towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach, with a row of more towers into the distance","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"DiscoA340"},"creditText":"DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg"}],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/hoa-vs-poa/"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Homeowners associations, property owners associations and condo regimes (horizontal property regimes) in South Carolina"},"keywords":"HOA vs POA, HOA vs POA South Carolina, is a POA the same as an HOA, property owners association South Carolina, regime fee South Carolina, horizontal property regime, condo association vs HOA, master association vs HOA, two HOA fees, HOA fees Myrtle Beach, Horizontal Property Act South Carolina"}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is an HOA the same as a POA in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Under the South Carolina Homeowners Association Act, a group that runs a neighborhood or a condo building is a homeowners association when recorded papers make the owners pay it dues, except a timeshare plan. A property owners association that meets that test is one too."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a POA put a lien on my house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, as an HOA can. About one in five of the more than 500 associations that recorded a lien for unpaid charges in Horry County in 2025 had \"property owners\" or \"POA\" in the name. A condo association can also take an unpaid fee to court and foreclose on the unit, the way a lender forecloses a mortgage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a POA voluntary in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only when no recorded papers make the owners pay it. A group with no such papers, such as a voluntary civic club, is not an HOA under South Carolina's HOA Act. If the papers on your home make you pay, the dues are not voluntary, whatever the group calls itself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a regime fee in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The condo fee. In South Carolina's condo law a condo is a horizontal property regime, so the fee its owners pay is called a regime fee."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do I own in a condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your unit alone, plus a share of the shared parts, given as a percentage in the condo's founding papers. The shared parts stay undivided; no owner can force them to be split up, and selling a unit sells its share with it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much are HOA fees on the Grand Strand?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For homes for sale, the typical monthly fee is about $640 for a condo, $275 for a townhouse and $95 for a house. About three in ten houses for sale have no association at all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a home have two HOAs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A home in a master-planned community can belong to a master association and to a neighborhood or condo association inside it, each with its own papers and fee. Grande Dunes, Barefoot Resort and Prince Creek each have more than one association in the county record."}}]}</script>
