pan></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated July 27, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Yes. In South Carolina law, a POA and an HOA are the same kind of group. The kind of home, condo or house, changes the fee.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. In South Carolina, a property owners association (POA) and a homeowners association (HOA) are the same thing in law. Under the South Carolina Homeowners Association Act, a group that runs a neighborhood or a condo building is an HOA when recorded papers make the owners pay it dues, whatever it calls itself. A timeshare plan is left out.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The kind of home changes the fee. The typical condo fee for sale here is about $640 a month, because the owners share the building and the association insures it. The typical house fee is about $95.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us an address. We find every association it belongs to.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us check a property</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px