}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:760px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only the name. A POA whose recorded papers make you pay dues can put a lien on your home if they go unpaid, as an HOA can. Associations here do it under both names. Of the more than 500 associations that recorded a lien for unpaid charges in Horry County in 2025, about half have "homeowners" or "HOA" in the name, and one in five "property owners" or "POA".</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="What Horry County associations call themselves"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">What Horry County associations call themselves</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Word in the name, for example</span><span role="columnheader">share of associations</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Homeowners or HOA</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.52" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">52%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Property owners or POA</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Another name</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">community or master association</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.18" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">18%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A condo word</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">regime, HPR or condominium</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.09" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">9%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the more than 500 associations that recorded a lien for unpaid charges in Horry County in 2025, from the county's deed index, with each name's spellings merged. The condo words include co-owners. A name with two label words is counted once. A few names with no label word are left out.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a condo, an unpaid fee is a lien on the unit under state law, and the association can foreclose it in court like a mortgage. For a house, the association's recorded papers say what it can do, and we read them for the home you send us. <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Can an HOA foreclose in South Carolina?</a> covers the steps, and <a href="/hoa/violations-and-fines/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA violations and fines</a> covers fines.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The name also does not tell you the kind of home. A few names mix a condo word with "property owners" or "homeowners". Some carry no label word at all, only a place name such as a Tower or Villas. The recorded papers say which kind it is.</p><p class="hp-note">On this page, POA means a property owners association, not a power of attorney.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="condo-vs-house"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo vs house">How is a condo association different from an HOA?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo association is an HOA too, but it runs a building, not a neighborhood. In a condo, the owners share the building. In a neighborhood of houses, you own your lot and the house on it. That one difference changes the fee, what it covers and how a lender looks at the home.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>House, townhouse or condo: what changes</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind of home</th><th scope="col">What you own</th><th scope="col">Roof and outside walls</th><th scope="col">Lender reviews the whole building</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>House in an HOA or POA</th><td data-label="What you own">The lot and the house</td><td data-label="Roof and outside walls">The HOA's rules say who</td><td data-label="Lender reviews the whole building">No full review</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span>Townhouse</th><td data-label="What you own">A lot or a unit, as the deed says</td><td data-label="Roof and outside walls">On a lot, HOA rules; as a unit, shared</td><td data-label="Lender reviews the whole building">On a lot, no; as a unit, like a condo</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Condo</th><td data-label="What you own">Your unit and a share of the building</td><td data-label="Roof and outside walls">Shared by every owner</td><td data-label="Lender reviews the whole building">Yes, if attached in a larger building</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building here becomes a condo when its owner records the condo's founding papers at the county.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Tall oceanfront towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach, with a row of more towers into the distance" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: DiscoA340. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: DiscoA340</a></span></div><figcaption>Oceanfront towers in Myrtle Beach.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-you-own"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What you own">What do you own, and who fixes the roof?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a condo you own your unit alone, plus a share of the shared parts, given as a percentage in the condo's founding papers.</p><figure class="hp-own"><div class="hp-own-g"><div class="pnl"><p class="k">A house in an HOA or POA</p><svg role="img" aria-label="Drawing: in a neighborhood of houses, you own the lot and the house on it; the association has the common areas." viewBox="0 0 340 250" width="100%" style="display:block;height:auto" font-family="var(--sans)"><rect x="0" y="210" width="340" height="40" fill="#e6dccb"/><rect x="8" y="96" width="190" height="130" rx="10" fill="rgba(196,120,58,.10)" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="7 5"/><polygon points="34,152 103,104 172,152" fill="#c4783a"/><rect x="46" y="150" width="114" height="62" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="92" y="174" width="22" height="38" fill="#1c2028" opacity=".85"/><rect x="58" y="164" width="22" height="18" fill="#cfdde6" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="126" y="164" width="22" height="18" fill="#cfdde6" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="216" y="180" width="116" height="30" rx="12" fill="#a9cbd8" stroke="#5c6b78" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="274" y="140" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="#1c2028" text-anchor="middle">Common</text><text x="274" y="164" font-size="20" fill="#1c2028" text-anchor="middle">areas</text><text x="8" y="30" font-size="23" font-weight="700" fill="#8a5226">Yours</text><text x="8" y="60" font-size="20" fill="#1c2028">the lot and the house on it</text></svg></div><div class="pnl"><p class="k">A condo</p><svg role="img" aria-label="Drawing: in a condo, you own your unit; the roof, main walls, halls, elevators and land are shared by every owner." viewBox="0 0 340 250" width="100%" style="display:block;height:auto" font-family="var(--sans)"><rect x="0" y="210" width="340" height="40" fill="#e6dccb"/><rect x="18" y="92" width="170" height="120" fill="rgba(92,107,120,.14)" stroke="#5c6b78" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="12" y="82" width="182" height="12" fill="#5c6b78"/><rect x="30" y="104" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="82" y="104" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="134" y="104" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="30" y="139" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="82" y="139" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="134" y="139" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#c4783a" stroke="#8a5226" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="30" y="174" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="82" y="174" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="134" y="174" width="42" height="26" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="176" y1="152" x2="214" y2="152" stroke="#8a5226" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="220" y="148" font-size="23" font-weight="700" fill="#8a5226">Yours</text><text x="220" y="174" font-size="20" fill="#1c2028">one unit</text><text x="8" y="26" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="#1c2028">Shared by every owner</text><text x="8" y="52" font-size="19" fill="#1c2028">roof, main walls, halls,</text><text x="8" y="74" font-size="19" fill="#1c2028">elevators and the land</text></svg></div></div><figcaption>Our drawing of what you own in each.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Unless the founding papers say otherwise, the shared parts include the land under the building, the foundations, main walls, roofs, halls, lobbies, stairways, elevators and the central power and water systems. Every owner pays a share of their upkeep by that percentage, and the association insures the building.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The association may also enter your unit at reasonable hours to repair the shared parts, and for emergency repairs. Your property tax bill is for your unit alone: if a neighbor does not pay, a tax sale cannot take your unit while your own taxes are paid.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">And in a neighborhood of houses?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HOA Act</a> has no rule on who maintains or insures a house in a planned community. The HOA's recorded rules say it. We read them for the home you send us and tell you what is yours to keep up.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="regime-fees"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Regime fees">What is a regime fee, and why is a condo fee higher?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A regime fee is a condo fee. In South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">condo law</a>, a condo is a horizontal property regime, and its name in its founding papers must end in "Horizontal Property Regime". A condo fee is higher because it pays for the building: the typical condo fee for sale here is nearly seven times the typical house fee.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Typical monthly association fee of homes for sale on the Grand Strand"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Typical monthly association fee of homes for sale on the Grand Strand</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home, range</span><span role="columnheader">typical fee a month</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">the middle half pay roughly $450 to $950</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $640</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Townhouse</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.43" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $275</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">House</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">the middle half pay roughly $50 to $150</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.148" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about $95</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of homes for sale in the local MLS with an association fee: 2,866 condos, 339 townhouses and 2,589 houses. The typical fee is the middle one: half pay more, half less.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a year, that is about $7,700 for the typical condo and about $1,100 for the typical house, on top of the mortgage, the property tax and your own insurance.</p><figure class="hp-pays"><p class="cap" id="hp-pays-cap">What the association fee covers</p><div class="hp-pays-t" role="table" aria-labelledby="hp-pays-cap"><div role="row" class="hd"><span role="columnheader">Covers</span><span role="columnheader">Condos</span><span role="columnheader">Town&shy;houses</span><span role="columnheader">Houses</span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Insurance</span><span role="cell"><b>78%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:78%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>34%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:34%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>4%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:4%"></i></i></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Water and sewer</span><span role="cell"><b>84%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:84%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>19%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:19%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>2%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:2%"></i></i></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Lawn care</span><span role="cell"><b>91%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:91%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>83%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:83%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>9%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:9%"></i></i></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Trash pickup</span><span role="cell"><b>91%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:91%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>73%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:73%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>46%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:46%"></i></i></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Internet</span><span role="cell"><b>70%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:70%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>25%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:25%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>9%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:9%"></i></i></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Shared amenities</span><span role="cell"><b>87%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:87%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>56%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:56%"></i></i></span><span role="cell"><b>61%</b><i aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:61%"></i></i></span></div></div><figcaption>Share of homes for sale with an association fee whose listing says the fee covers each item. Our count of the local MLS.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Compare the whole yearly cost of each home, not the two fees. On most houses, the insurance policy is a bill of its own on top of the fee. About three in ten houses for sale have no association at all; <a href="/buyers/no-hoa-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes with no HOA</a> has its own page.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="two-associations"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Two associations">Why do some homes pay two HOA fees?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Because some master-planned communities have two layers. A master association has rules over the whole community. Inside it, a neighborhood association or a condo association can have its own rules and its own fee. For you, that can mean two bills and two sets of rules. The name will not warn you: only 4 of the more than 500 associations in the lien count above have "master" in the name.</p><figure class="hp-layers" aria-label="Diagram: a master association over a whole community, with a neighborhood association and a condo association inside it"><div class="l1"><b>Master association</b><span>Rules over the whole community, and its own fee</span><div class="l2s"><div class="l2"><b>Neighborhood association</b><span>Its own rules and fee for one neighborhood</span></div><div class="l2"><b>Condo association</b><span>Its own rules and fee for one building</span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our diagram of two layers. A home can belong to the master association and to one association inside it.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county record shows which associations a community has, but not what each one charges, and the seller's disclosure form has room for one association. For a home in a community like these, we find each association and its fee before you make an offer.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Three communities here with more than one association</h3><div class="hp-comms"><article class="hp-comm"><h3>Grande Dunes<span>Myrtle Beach</span></h3><ul class="chips"><li class="top">Grande Dunes Master Association</li><li class="in">Bal Harbor Property Owners Association</li><li class="in">Cipriana Park Property Owners Association</li></ul><p>The master association covers the whole community, and a cost-sharing agreement for its common areas is on file. Bal Harbor and Cipriana Park, two neighborhoods inside it, each have their own POA. The county record shows an agreement on architectural review, the approval of changes to a home's outside, between the master and each of the two.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Grande%20Dunes">Ask about Grande Dunes<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></article><article class="hp-comm"><h3>Barefoot Resort<span>North Myrtle Beach</span></h3><ul class="chips"><li class="top">Barefoot Resort Residential Owners Association</li><li class="in">Barefoot Resort Nonresidential Owners Association</li><li class="in">Condo associations for its buildings</li></ul><p>The residential owners association is the master. It records the design guidelines and the policy for adding new communities. A separate nonresidential association has its own papers. Condo buildings inside Barefoot have their own condo associations.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Barefoot%20Resort">Ask about Barefoot Resort<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></article><article class="hp-comm"><h3>Prince Creek<span>Murrells Inlet</span></h3><ul class="chips"><li class="top">Prince Creek Master Community Association</li><li class="in">Prince Creek West Road and Park Districts Association</li></ul><p>The master association records the master rules and design standards. Prince Creek West also has an association for its roads and parks, with its own rules and its own policy for collecting late dues.</p><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Prince%20Creek">Ask about Prince Creek<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></article></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="surprises"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Surprises">What surprises buyers in an HOA or a condo?</h2><ol class="hp-sur"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><div><b>Insuring the wrong thing</b><p>In a condo, the association insures the building. You may insure your unit on your own policy. Flood insurance has a cap: the association's <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CFR-2025-title44-vol1/pdf/CFR-2025-title44-vol1-part61-appA3.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Flood Insurance Program</a> policy and your own pay no more than $250,000 together for your unit, and a loss is paid once.</p><p class="do">We read the association's policy before you buy yours, so you know what your own policy has to cover. <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Master insurance and your condo policy</a> has the split.</p></div></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div><b>Finding a second association after closing</b><p>The <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Residential%20Property%20Condition%20Disclosure%20Statement%20Addendum.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">seller's disclosure form</a> has room for one association's charges and one contact. The <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeName" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Register of Deeds</a> index is free to search online, but the county does not show the papers themselves to the public online. They are viewed and printed in Conway, or ordered by mail.</p><p class="do">We find every association on the home, and each one's fee, before you make an offer.</p></div></li></ol><div class="hp-stat"><p class="k">The papers often come late</p><p class="big">65%</p><p class="sub">of Horry County owners who complained to the state about their association, and said when they got its papers, got them after they bought.</p><p class="do">We read each association's papers before you make an offer.</p><p class="src">235 of 359 complaints to <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit">South Carolina Consumer Affairs</a>, 2022 to 2025</p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we check">What do we check before you make an offer?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When we search for an investor's rental, we leave out communities that ban renting and read the HOA papers for rules that would hurt the rental plan. <a href="/hoa/rental-restrictions/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA rental restrictions</a> covers those rules, and <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA reserves</a> covers what an association has saved.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">What you get when we check a property</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Every association the property belongs to, what each one charges, what each fee covers, and the rules that would change your plans. Send us the address.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check a property</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Loans">Does a condo association change your loan?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for many condos. A loan that follows <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-01/general-information-project-standards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae's rules</a> on an attached condo unit in a building of more than ten units needs a review of the whole condo project first. A house or townhouse in a neighborhood where every owner must belong to the HOA, a detached condo and a unit in a small condo building skip the full review. <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Condos most lenders will not lend on</a> covers buildings that fail it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="pros-and-cons"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Pros and cons">What are the pros and cons of an HOA vs a POA?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The real choice is between a condo and a house, not between an HOA and a POA:</p><div class="hp-pc"><div class="hp-pc-c"><h3>A condo</h3><ul><li class="g"><span aria-hidden="true">+</span><span>The association keeps up and insures the building, roof included.</span></li><li class="g"><span aria-hidden="true">+</span><span>Your property tax bill is for your unit alone.</span></li><li class="b"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span><span>The biggest monthly fee, because it pays for the building.</span></li><li class="b"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span><span>Every owner shares the cost of big repairs to the building.</span></li><li class="b"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span><span>A lender may review the whole building before your loan.</span></li></ul></div><div class="hp-pc-c"><h3>A house in an HOA or POA</h3><ul><li class="g"><span aria-hidden="true">+</span><span>The smallest monthly fee.</span></li><li class="g"><span aria-hidden="true">+</span><span>The lot and the house are yours alone.</span></li><li class="b"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span><span>Few house fees include insurance, so the policy on the house is yours to buy.</span></li><li class="b"><span aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span><span>A second association, with its own rules and fee, in some master-planned communities.</span></li></ul></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Whether dues are tax deductible has its own page: <a href="/hoa/tax-deductible/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">are HOA fees tax deductible</a>. How owners split the bill for a big condo repair is on <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo special assessments</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="hoa-vs-poa-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">HOA vs POA questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is an HOA the same as a POA in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Under the South Carolina Homeowners Association Act, a group that runs a neighborhood or a condo building is a homeowners association when recorded papers make the owners pay it dues, except a timeshare plan. A property owners association that meets that test is one too.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a POA put a lien on my house?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, as an HOA can. About one in five of the more than 500 associations that recorded a lien for unpaid charges in Horry County in 2025 had "property owners" or "POA" in the name. A condo association can also take an unpaid fee to court and foreclose on the unit, the way a lender forecloses a mortgage.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is a POA voluntary in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only when no recorded papers make the owners pay it. A group with no such papers, such as a voluntary civic club, is not an HOA under South Carolina's HOA Act. If the papers on your home make you pay, the dues are not voluntary, whatever the group calls itself.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a regime fee in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The condo fee. In South Carolina's condo law a condo is a horizontal property regime, so the fee its owners pay is called a regime fee.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What do I own in a condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Your unit alone, plus a share of the shared parts, given as a percentage in the condo's founding papers. The shared parts stay undivided; no owner can force them to be split up, and selling a unit sells its share with it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much are HOA fees on the Grand Strand?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For homes for sale, the typical monthly fee is about $640 for a condo, $275 for a townhouse and $95 for a house. About three in ten houses for sale have no association at all.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a home have two HOAs?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A home in a master-planned community can belong to a master association and to a neighborhood or condo association inside it, each with its own papers and fee. Grande Dunes, Barefoot Resort and Prince Creek each have more than one association in the county record.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-30</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Residential%20Property%20Condition%20Disclosure%20Statement%20Addendum.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure addendum</a>, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CFR-2025-title44-vol1/pdf/CFR-2025-title44-vol1-part61-appA3.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">44 CFR 61 App. A(3)</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-01/general-information-project-standards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-01</a>, <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDCA HOA reports</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeName" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>. Fees and what they cover: our count of homes for sale in the local MLS. Association names: our count of the county's lien index. Not legal advice; a South Carolina attorney can advise on your documents.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Oceanfront towers in Myrtle Beach. Photo: DiscoA340, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped the photo to the towers and the beach (most of the sky cut), resized it, and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 crops for the page's share image.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them. Each photo keeps the license listed with it.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-hoa-vs-poa/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Oceanfront towers in Myrtle Beach","caption":"Oceanfront towers in Myrtle Beach.","description":"Tall oceanfront towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach, with a row of more towers into the distance","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"DiscoA340"},"creditText":"DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© DiscoA340, CC BY-SA 4.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront_2.jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"oceanfront, seen from the beach, Myrtle Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Know every association before you make an offer</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Send us the address. We find each association it belongs to, read its documents and tell you what each one charges and what it covers.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us check a property</a></div></section></main>
